You are writing AgentMark, a general-purpose programming language written as Markdown: files ending in .agent.md that `agentmark build` compiles to a Rust binary (records, enums, functions, commands, services, collection words, a built-in library, files). Agents that ask an AI model are one feature of it. You are not writing Rust.

SOURCE OF TRUTH
Read docs/reference.md of the AgentMark checkout before writing anything. It is the whole language in one file: every declaration, statement form and error message, with tested examples. If it does not show a construct, the construct does not exist; ask the human instead of inventing syntax. If this project lives outside the checkout, keep a copy of that file next to the project and read that.

WORKFLOW
1. Edit one .agent.md file.
2. Run `agentmark check .` right after every edit and fix every error before doing anything else. The messages say what is wrong and often what to write.
3. Run `agentmark fmt .` (canonical style; `agentmark fmt --check .` only reports).
4. Run `agentmark lint .` and fix the warnings.
5. Run and test: `agentmark run . -- <arguments>` or `agentmark trace . -- <arguments>` (the arguments after `--` go to the program). A program with a model or outside tools adds `--mock --fixtures fixtures/<name>`. Compare output and traces, not generated Rust.
6. `agentmark doc .` prints what each command, function, agent and block can touch.

RULES
- Never write Rust, except inside a `## Rust: <name>` block the human asked for. Never edit generated code (target/ or an -o output folder); change the .agent.md source.
- Do not edit rust/connect.rs beyond the tool implementations the human asked for.
- Effect lines in bindings/*.agent.md are for humans to approve. Never change `- effect: unverified` there, and never loosen a block's tools to silence `check`. Show the human what the library can touch and let them approve it. --allow-unverified is for exploring, not for finishing.
- A `## Rust` block needs an `- effect:` line. Declare the honest class (never a weaker one to pass `check`) and tell the human, because that line is trusted, not verified.
- One statement per line, at most 120 characters, at most 3 levels of nesting. Put a `>` description above every record, enum, function, command, tool, rule and agent.
- In an agent, follow every `ask model` with a `guard:` on its answer. Name a money, duration or URL literal used more than once as a `# Constant`.
- Tools outside the program come from tools.toml; do not use a tool or verb that is not listed there. The `file` tool and the library (text, regex, number, money, date, path, json, csv) are built in. A helper you can write in AgentMark is a `# Tool` or `# Function`.
- No secrets in files; keys come from the environment (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY).

WHERE THINGS LIVE
agentmark.toml (name, version, targets, [lint], [models], [rust.dependencies]); tools.toml (tool manifest); main.agent.md (imports and one `# Run`); records/, functions/, commands/, services/, rules/, agents/ (one declaration per file); bindings/ (generated by `agentmark bind`, reviewed by a human); fixtures/ (model answers, tool data, expected traces); rust/connect.rs (real tool implementations). A new command, service or agent is a file plus an import and a line in the `# Run` of main.agent.md; a project with exactly one command is started as `my-tool <arguments>`.

If you are stuck, say what you tried and what `check` answered. Do not work around the checker.
